<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-16"?>
<!--
  ATAP-ElevatedInstallBroker (Task 13.76.g / plan Task 4.2.b)

  Runs Invoke-ElevationBrokerRequest.ps1 as SvcAnsibleAdmin with highest privileges so
  unelevated agents can complete AllUsers installs without any UAC interaction.

  REGISTRATION IS HITL: it needs administrator rights and the SvcAnsibleAdmin credential
  from Bitwarden (SecretName SvcAnsibleAdmin.utat01, provisioned by
  Task 13.76.f). Do not register this task until 13.76.f has completed.

  Placeholders to replace before registering:
    __BROKER_ROOT__      C:\ProgramData\ATAP\ElevationBroker
    __BROKER_SCRIPT__    <BrokerRoot>\bin\Invoke-ElevationBrokerRequest.ps1
    __SERVICE_ACCOUNT__  .\SvcAnsibleAdmin  (or DOMAIN\SvcAnsibleAdmin)

  Trigger choice (revised 2026-07-28). The original registration used a repeating
  one-minute time trigger, explicitly REJECTING an event/folder-watch trigger because a
  folder watcher needs an ETW/WMI subscription on a world-readable directory, i.e. more
  attack surface than draining a directory the broker already ACL-checks. That reasoning
  still holds and is NOT reversed here: there is still no watcher and no subscription.

  What changed is that the timer was measured. Between 2026-07-25 and 2026-07-28 it fired
  ~4,644 times to service 4 real requests (0.086% hit rate); every empty drain paid a full
  profiled pwsh start (~6 CPU-sec), contributing to sustained CPU saturation on utat01.

  The request itself is now the event. Request-ElevatedInstall.ps1 starts this task on
  demand immediately after staging a request, which AllowStartOnDemand (already true here)
  has always permitted. This is NOT a watcher: the task's Action is fixed in this
  registration, so a caller granted start rights cannot influence WHAT runs. It can only ask
  the broker to drain a folder it already validates. The escalation surface is unchanged.

  BootTrigger is retained deliberately, as the catch-up path for a request staged while the
  broker could not run (crash, reboot, revoked start rights). It is the only remaining
  unattended drain.

  MultipleInstancesPolicy MUST be Queue, not IgnoreNew. Under -Once the broker enumerates
  the requests folder exactly once and exits. With IgnoreNew, a request staged after a
  running instance enumerated would have its start request DROPPED and would sit unprocessed
  until the next boot. The one-minute timer used to mask that race; without the timer it
  would surface as an intermittent silent hang. Queue makes the second start wait and run.

  Companion: Grant-ElevationBrokerStartRights.ps1 grants the build account the Read+Execute
  rights that the on-demand start requires against this task. The client starts it through the
  Schedule.Service COM API, because the ScheduledTasks PowerShell module is not present under
  PowerShell 7 on utat01 (so Start-ScheduledTask is not available there).
-->
<Task version="1.4" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>ATAP elevated install broker. Executes hash-pinned canonical installers requested by unelevated agents via C:\ProgramData\ATAP\ElevationBroker\requests. Task 13.76.g.</Description>
    <URI>\ATAP\ATAP-ElevatedInstallBroker</URI>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <!--
      No TimeTrigger. The task is started on demand by Request-ElevatedInstall.ps1 the
      moment a request is staged, so a periodic drain would be pure waste (see header).
      BootTrigger is the catch-up path for requests staged while the broker could not run.
    -->
    <BootTrigger>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    </BootTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <UserId>__SERVICE_ACCOUNT__</UserId>
      <LogonType>Password</LogonType>
      <RunLevel>HighestAvailable</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <!-- Queue, NOT IgnoreNew. See the race documented in the header. -->
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>Queue</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <StartWhenAvailable>true</StartWhenAvailable>
    <RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>false</RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>
    <IdleSettings>
      <StopOnIdleEnd>false</StopOnIdleEnd>
      <RestartOnIdle>false</RestartOnIdle>
    </IdleSettings>
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>
    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <Hidden>false</Hidden>
    <RunOnlyIfIdle>false</RunOnlyIfIdle>
    <WakeToRun>false</WakeToRun>
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT1H</ExecutionTimeLimit>
    <Priority>7</Priority>
  </Settings>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>pwsh.exe</Command>
      <Arguments>-NoLogo -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "__BROKER_SCRIPT__" -BrokerRoot "__BROKER_ROOT__" -Once</Arguments>
      <WorkingDirectory>__BROKER_ROOT__</WorkingDirectory>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
